      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты же её уволила.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'You fired her.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to fire'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Она сама уволилась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'She quit herself.' Kristina delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'quit'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какая разница?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What difference does it make?' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'what' directly with 'difference', combining 'what' with 'difference'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я предлагаю сделку.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I propose a deal.' Chef Barinov chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'suggest' for the ongoing action, followed by 'deal'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Какую?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What kind?' Kristina asks this with a single word meaning 'what', using rising intonation to turn it into a quick question.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я буду делать суши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will make sushi.' Chef Barinov chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'will be' for the ongoing action, followed by 'do' and 'sushi'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А ты вернёшь Вику.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And you will bring Vika back.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'will get back'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Это смешно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is funny.' Kristina delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'ridiculous'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Гости ждут.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'The customers are waiting.' Chef Barinov puts the word for 'visitors' together with 'are waiting' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Хорошо.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Fine.' A crisp, everyday conversational reply meaning 'good' that you will hear constantly in Russian dialogue.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я согласна.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I agree.' Kristina delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'agree'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вот и отлично.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'That is excellent.' Chef Barinov delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'perfect'.",
